39,2,1,talk,Takumi,,- **Takumi**: The bank statements show a third of renewals needed chasing. We cannot set a fee on money that isn't reliably there.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"- **Asuka**: @Takumi, that confirms my fear. If a third of renewals need chasing, the current system is already failing. We need a structure that doesn't rely on constant reminders.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"- **Benjamin**: @Takumi, the chasing is the problem. My hours show six months of work for event charges versus one for annual. We must stop the bleeding.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"- **Jonathan**: @Benjamin, the hours are one thing, but the sign-in sheets show a quarter of us doing two-thirds of the work. That imbalance is the real issue.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"- **Shunichi**: @Jonathan, the imbalance is real, but the hesitation is the symptom. My data shows turnout dropped a third after we tried charging per event.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Benjamin, six hours a month is unacceptable. @Shunichi, that drop proves activity-based charging kills participation. We need a flat fee to stop the chasing.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"- **Asuka**: @Takumi, a flat fee ignores the newcomers who fear a full-year commitment. @Shunichi, your data proves per-event charges scare people away.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Takumi"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, the taster forms show they fear the commitment, not the fee. @Takumi, the flat fee is the only way to stop the six-hour monthly grind.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"- **Jonathan**: @Benjamin, the hours are just one part. I need to see the sign-in sheets to prove who is actually carrying the load.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, the sign-in sheets show who attends, not why they hesitate. My data proves the hesitation is about the cost structure, not the people.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, the sign-in sheets prove the imbalance, but they don't fix the cash flow. @Benjamin, if the flat fee stops the chasing, we can finally budget properly.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, the commitment fear is real. @Shunichi, the hesitation is about visibility. We need a structure that protects the quiet ones while keeping the books simple.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, simplicity is the only way to save the treasurer. @Takumi, the flat fee is the only option that fits the data.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, simplicity helps the books, but it ignores the eighteen people who never show up. Why should they pay the same as the nine who do everything?","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, the eighteen who never show up are the ones who need the door open. If we make them pay for what they don’t use, they’ll vanish entirely.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, the eighteen are the risk. If we charge per event, they vanish and we lose the base. A flat fee keeps them in, even if they don’t use it.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
